ECOSYSTEM POLICY CHANGE, human-directed by Zach 2026-07-28 (interactive /ideate, filed to scheduler as a36d3c0): **dexter is now the default execution host -- policy is to move everything possible there.** This reverses the "hardware-evidenced pinning only" rule from the 2026-07-24 dexter bring-up. Relevant to realisateur specifically because /ideate and /nightly-batch both triage across projects and both currently reason from the OLD rule: `schedule/_paced.dexter.conf` lines 29-33 still say "only hardware/network-evidenced projects belong here. Do NOT move a project here just to balance load", and that file is dexter-owned so it cannot be corrected from mandark -- an agent reading it will follow a retired policy until someone amends it in a human session on dexter. Treat "should this project move to dexter?" as answered YES-by-default in triage from now on, with the burden of proof on staying, not on moving. THE CONSTRAINT THAT SURVIVES, and that triage should now check for every project rather than only for hardware-pinned ones: dexter must be able to reach that project s git remote. wtul was moved on 2026-07-25 and reverted the same day because dexter had no `github-wtul-deploy` SSH alias and no deploy key, so `git ls-remote` failed at name resolution. Under the old policy that gap surfaced once, for one project. Under the new one it is the rate limiter for the whole migration, and every project with a `git@<alias>:` style REPO_URL has the same latent gap. Worth a survey: which registered projects use an SSH host alias in REPO_URL, and which of those aliases exist on dexter? That is an offline-answerable question on the mandark side (grep the confs) and a one-command check on the dexter side (`git ls-remote` per remote), and doing it as a batch before the moves start would convert a series of same-day reverts into one provisioning pass. Also note gardien is separately deployed on dexter and has a real backup running there since 2026-07-28, so dexter is no longer an unproven host.
